    {
      "id": "rtdb.semantic.validate-non-cascade",
      "kind": "semantic",
      "engine": "rtdb",
      "reference": "https://firebase.google.com/docs/database/security/core-syntax#validation-rules",
      "status": "unprobed",
      "note": "Production evaluates a `.validate` rule on ANCESTORS of the written path too, against the merged post-write value at that ancestor: writing `/p1/sub/k1` under a parent whose rule is `newData.hasChildren(['x'])` is DENIED, while the same write under a rule-free ancestor is allowed, and is allowed again once `/p1/x` exists. So the non-cascade property means only that a `.validate` never GRANTS (unlike a truthy ancestor `.read`/`.write`); it is NOT skipped on ancestors, and every applicable rule must pass. The simulator collects `.validate` rules from the write location DOWNWARD only and therefore allows writes production denies (a false ALLOW). Captured by rules-rtdb-r15-validate-ancestor-scope; divergence documented on registry row rtdb-rules#15 and pinned in the replay suite."
    },
